                "firstName": "A. R.",
                "lastName": "Cenfetelli"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "The growing diversity and increasing complexity of new applications demand more efficient data-handling devices and methods. The computer designers have responded with a large complement of different input/output (I/O) and auxiliary storage devices. However, the majority of users in the small-to-intermediate computer class desire to spend most, if not all, of their programming effort in solving their own problems. They wish to expend a minimum of effort in coping with the intricacies of the various I/O devices, or in developing new techniques for retrieval of information. Thed ata management facilities for DOS/360 and TOS/360, discussed in this paper,<sup xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">1</sup> have been designed especially for IBM SYSTEM/360 users in this class.",
